course: dictionary
lesson: 15273
title: Signified: showed, meant or stood for
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Signified is the past form of the verb signify. To signify means to show, to mean or to stand for something. Use the clues in the sentence to work out which meaning fits.
explain: «The dark clouds signified danger.» The clouds did not say the word danger, but they showed that danger was coming. Ask yourself: what did the thing in the sentence show or stand for?
explain: Signified can also mean made known by a sign or an action. «She signified that she agreed by nodding.» She said nothing, but her nod showed she agreed. Try swapping in showed or meant: if the sentence still makes sense, you have the right meaning.
fact: Signify means to show, mean or stand for something.
fact: Signified is the past form, so it tells us about something that has already happened.
fact: «The fall of the wall signified the end of an era.» Here it means showed that an era had ended.
fact: In language study, the signified is the idea or meaning a word or sign points to. The signifier is its sound or letters.
exercise: «The dark clouds signified danger.» What does signified mean here? | Hid the storm from view | Washed the danger away | Made the sky very bright | *Showed that danger was coming
exercise: «She signified that she agreed by nodding.» How did she show that she agreed? | By shouting loudly | By writing a long letter | *By nodding | By leaving the room
exercise: In language study, what is the signified? | The name of a book | The last word in a sentence | The sound or letters of a word | *The idea or meaning a word or sign points to
test: «The red light signified that the train would stop.» What does signified mean? | Painted the train red | Forgot about the train | *Showed that the train would stop | Made the train go faster
test: Which word is the past form of signify? | *signified | signifying | signal | signifies
exam: «The raised hand signified agreement.» What does signified mean? | *Showed that they agreed | Asked for a break | Was lost in the crowd | Dropped the hand onto the table
